## sess128 — ruling blocker 1 landed on 0.11.444

Tree `0.11.443` → **0.11.444**, srcversion `3BBBB5F36CECFDD3A90159F`, builds clean. Not deployed; fleet stays on 0.11.440. **Still STOP-SHIP** — 4 of the 7 sess126 blockers remain.

I continued D-SAMENODE-WAITER-CANCEL-COLLISION (ledger #16) rather than starting at #1, same reason as sess119–127: the DLM core is mid-landing and sess126 set the order, which put blocker 1 next.

**Blocker 1 — the drain had no time bound.** An absolute `mxfs_pal_time_ms()` deadline now runs `sweep → dispatch → resolve → find_slot_skip` and `sweep → dispatch → drop_own_waiter → read_slot/caw_slot/sleep`, checked before each claim, each mode, each slot read, each CAW and each retry sleep. The 1000-attempt loop is gone entirely — it was never a time bound, and one drain sweep of 16 entries × 4 modes × that loop ran on the order of 512s against a 2000ms budget. `find_slot` returns `-ETIMEDOUT` rather than `-ENOENT` on expiry, kept distinct on purpose: the terminal retraction proof requires a *complete* walk, and a truncated one can't tell "absent" from "not reached".

Two details worth naming. First, the non-collector budget can't be a single constant: with a registry present the worker owns persistence and the XFS thread should leave in 200ms, but `ctx->lreq` can genuinely be NULL at runtime (an lreq allocation failure at start leaves the whole mechanism inert and the mount still proceeds), and then the calling thread is the only collector that bit will ever get — so the deadline is derived *inside* the function, after it knows whether the obligation was recorded. Second, an entry the drain claimed but never attempted is now released without a failure count or a backoff re-arm; charging a retry to work that never ran walks a healthy obligation toward the P253 bar for free.

I did **not** paper over the ruling's NOTE. Block-layer I/O already in flight is not cancellable, so DRAIN_MS bounds the work *started*, not wall time. The header block, the worker comment and the P254 line all say that explicitly, and P245/P252 now name rc=-110 as the budget rather than an I/O error.

**One thing I got wrong and corrected mid-session:** I wrote a comment claiming the new single-claim drain cap makes the sweep round-robin, then traced it and found it false — `ctx->lreq_sweep` stores a *bucket* index, so the next sweep restarts at that chain's head and re-claims the same entry forever. Blocker 4 is confirmed real and unfixable by any cap tweak; it needs the ruling's owed-ready queue. I removed the comment and wrote the full queue design (link fields, the pending⟺queued invariant, and why weakening it makes `lreq_gc` a use-after-free) into ccmemory for the next landing.

Next: blocker 4 (the queue), then 2+3 together as one lifecycle change. Blocker 7 still needs its false-positive check against `dlm_caw.c:2105`.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 29 open defects, 17 critical, DLM core under two stacked stop-ship rulings. Marker unwritten.

